README.md
    10→# `htmy`
    12→**Async**, **pure-Python** server-side rendering engine.
    14→Unleash your creativity with the full power and Python, without the hassle of learning a new templating language or dealing with its limitations!
    16→## Key features
    …
    18→- **Async**-first, to let you make the best use of modern async tools.
    19→- **Powerful**, React-like **context support**, so you can avoid prop-drilling.
    20→- Sync and async **function components** with **decorator syntax**.
    21→- All baseline **HTML** tags built-in.
    22→- **Async HTML streaming** support for optimal TTFB (time to first byte).
    23→- Support for **native HTML/XML** documents with dynamic formatting and **slot rendering**, **without custom syntax**.
    24→- **Markdown** support with tools for customization.
    25→- Async, JSON based **internationalization**.
    26→- Built-in, easy to use `ErrorBoundary` component for graceful error handling.
    27→- **Unopinionated**: use the backend, CSS, and JS frameworks of your choice, the way you want to use them.
    28→- Everything is **easily customizable**, from the rendering engine to components, formatting and context management.
    29→- Automatic and customizable **property-name conversion** from snake case to kebab case.
    30→- **Compatible** with any other templating library through wrappers.
    31→- **Fully-typed**.
    33→## Testimonials
    …
    47→## Support
    …
    49→Consider supporting the development and maintenance of the project through [sponsoring](https://buymeacoffee.com/volfpeter), or reach out for [consulting](https://www.volfp.com/contact?subject=Consulting%20-%20HTMY) so you can get the most out of the library.
    51→## Installation
    …
    53→The package is available on PyPI and can be installed with:
    55→```console
    56→$ pip install htmy
    57→```
    59→The package has the following optional dependencies:
    61→- `lxml` _(recommended)_: When installed, it is prioritized over `xml.etree.ElementTree` and provides more secure, faster, and more flexible HTML and XML processing. It is used, for example, for Markdown processing. Install with: `pip install "htmy[lxml]"`.
    63→## Concepts
    …
    65→The entire library -- from the rendering engine itself to the built-in components -- is built around a few simple protocols and a handful of simple utility classes. This means that you can easily customize, extend, or replace basically everything in the library. Yes, even the rendering engine. The remaining parts will keep working as expected.
    67→Also, the library doesn't rely on advanced Python features such as metaclasses or descriptors. There are also no complex base classes and the like. Even a junior engineer could understand, develop, and debug an application that's built with `htmy`.
